pgg-c02-e04-i12	the affordable laptop; an affordable laptop	Accusative: the requested item is the direct object; a masculine adjective takes -en after either article (§§2.2, 6.1).
pgg-c02-e04-i13	the large dictionary; a large dictionary	Accusative direct object: a neuter adjective takes -e after the definite article and -es after the indefinite (§§2.2, 6.1).
pgg-c02-e04-i14	the blue felt-tip pen; a blue felt-tip pen	Accusative: the requested item is the direct object; a masculine adjective takes -en after either article (§§2.2, 6.1).
pgg-c02-e04-i15	the new telephone; a new telephone	Accusative direct object: a neuter adjective takes -e after the definite article and -es after the indefinite (§§2.2, 6.1).
pgg-c02-e04-i16	the challenging novel; a challenging novel	Accusative: the requested item is the direct object; a masculine adjective takes -en after either article (§§2.2, 6.1).
pgg-c02-e04-i17	the comfortable armchairs; comfortable armchairs	Accusative: the requested items are direct objects; a plural adjective takes -en after the definite article and -e without one (§§2.2, 6.1).
pgg-c02-e04-i18	the electric razor; an electric razor	Accusative: the requested item is the direct object; a masculine adjective takes -en after either article (§§2.2, 6.1).
pgg-c02-e04-i19	the patterned tights; patterned tights	Accusative: the requested items are direct objects; a plural adjective takes -en after the definite article and -e without one (§§2.2, 6.1).
pgg-c02-e04-i20	the juicy oranges; juicy oranges	Accusative: the requested items are direct objects; a plural adjective takes -en after the definite article and -e without one (§§2.2, 6.1).
pgg-c02-e04-i21	the large room; a large room	Accusative direct object: a neuter adjective takes -e after the definite article and -es after the indefinite (§§2.2, 6.1).
pgg-c02-e04-i22	the gray scarf; a gray scarf	Accusative: the requested item is the direct object; a masculine adjective takes -en after either article (§§2.2, 6.1).
pgg-c02-e05-i1	They had to walk the long way.	Accusative: a bare noun phrase with a motion verb marks the distance travelled (§2.2.2).
pgg-c02-e05-i2	Everyone at the office got along well, but after work each person went their separate way again.	Genitive: the route is expressed by a fixed adverbial phrase meaning to go one's way (§2.3.3).
pgg-c02-e05-i3	I still haven't seen him at all today.	Accusative: a bare adverbial time phrase marks the full duration (§2.2.2).
pgg-c02-e05-i5	We were on vacation for almost the whole of August last year.	Accusative: bare time phrases mark a specific period and a complete duration; the year phrase omits its article (§2.2.2).
pgg-c02-e05-i6	One day, I hope to travel all the way down the Rhine.	Genitive marks an indefinite day; accusative with the motion verb marks the distance travelled (§§2.3.3, 2.2.2).
pgg-c02-e05-i7	He has already been skiing four times this winter.	Accusative: a bare time phrase identifies a specific period (§2.2.2).
pgg-c02-e05-i8	One evening, as he was just going down the stairs, the doorbell suddenly rang.	Genitive marks an indefinite evening; accusative with the direction verb marks the path travelled (§§2.3.3, 2.2.2).
pgg-c02-e05-i9	Cut the dough into slices one centimeter thick and chill them for a moment.	Accusative: the first bare phrase measures thickness and the second measures duration (§2.2.2).
pgg-c02-e05-i10	A spider is crawling up your arm right now.	Accusative: the bare noun phrase with a direction adverb marks the path travelled (§2.2.2).
pgg-c02-e05-i11	After he had waited almost a month for the package, it finally arrived one fine morning.	Accusative marks duration; genitive marks an indefinite morning in a set time expression (§§2.2.2, 2.3.3).
pgg-c02-e12-i1	Tobias looks like his brother.	Dative: an adjective expressing similarity takes the person used for comparison as its complement (§2.5.4).
pgg-c02-e12-i2	The boy is too cold in the water.	Dative: an impersonal sensation construction marks the experiencer in the dative (§2.5.4).
pgg-c02-e12-i3	The red dress fits the young woman very well.	Dative: the person concerned or affected by how something fits is marked in the dative (§2.5).
pgg-c02-e12-i4	The woman washes her hands.	Dative: a reflexive possessor precedes the body part, which takes the definite article (§2.5.3).
pgg-c02-e12-i5	You put on the green sweater.	Dative: the subject's reflexive pronoun marks possession of the article of clothing (§2.5.3).
pgg-c02-e12-i6	Maditha cut her finger.	Dative: a body part used with a preposition requires the subject's reflexive possessor (§2.5.3).
pgg-c02-e12-i7	Niklas broke his leg.	Dative: the subject's reflexive pronoun marks possession of the affected body part (§2.5.3).
pgg-c02-e12-i8	This perfume was much too expensive for her acquaintance.	Dative: with an adjective qualified by 'too,' the person whose standpoint is expressed precedes the adjective (§2.5.4).
pgg-c02-e12-i9	I poured my colleague some more wine.	Dative: the recipient is the indirect object, alongside the direct object denoting what is poured (§2.5.1).
pgg-c02-e12-i10	Then my father dropped the house key through the grate.	Dative: the father is the person adversely affected when the key falls through the grate (§2.5.2).
pgg-c02-e12-i11	Roe deer crossed the hiker's path.	Dative: the hiker is the person affected by the deer crossing his path (§2.5.2).
pgg-c02-e12-i12	This skirt wasn't long enough for my sister.	Dative: with an adjective qualified by 'enough,' the person whose standpoint is expressed precedes the adjective (§2.5.4).
pgg-c02-e12-i13	Andreas brought his girlfriend a piece of cake.	Dative: the recipient is the indirect object, alongside the direct object denoting what is brought (§2.5.1).
pgg-c02-e12-i14	Time passed much too slowly for the children.	Dative: the children supply the standpoint from which the rate of an event is judged (§2.5.2).
pgg-c02-e12-i15	The child's stomach hurt.	Dative: the affected child is the possessor of the body part and is not the sentence's subject (§2.5.3).
pgg-c02-e12-i16	The mother put the trousers on her daughter.	Dative: the daughter is the possessor of the clothing and is not the sentence's subject (§2.5.3).
pgg-c02-e12-i17	The fat man wiped the sweat from his forehead.	Dative: a reflexive possessor precedes the body part, which takes the definite article (§2.5.3).
pgg-c02-e10-i1	The car is too expensive for my sister.	Dative: the person whose standpoint is expressed precedes an adjective qualified by 'too' (§2.5.2).
pgg-c02-e10-i2	You'll surely watch the film.	Dative: the reflexive benefactive marks the person for whose own benefit the action is done (§2.5.2).
pgg-c02-e10-i3	The plate fell from her hand.	Dative: the person is adversely affected by the plate falling from her possession (§2.5.2).
pgg-c02-e10-i4	England is too cold for him.	Dative: the person whose standpoint is expressed precedes an adjective qualified by 'too' (§2.5.2).
pgg-c02-e10-i5	She wrote down his address for me.	Dative: the benefactive marks the person on whose behalf the action is done (§2.5.2).
pgg-c02-e10-i6	It was an honor for us to spend the evening with you.	Dative: with a copular verb and noun, the person concerned is marked in the dative (§2.5.2).
pgg-c02-e10-i7	Children eat you out of house and home.	Dative: the person is disadvantaged by the undesirable effect of the action (§2.5.2).
pgg-c02-e10-i8	The girl wrecks the boy's sandcastle.	Dative: the boy is disadvantaged by an action affecting his possession (§2.5.2).
pgg-c02-e09-i1	the effect of some alcohol	The <i>von</i>-construction is usual because the dependent noun follows an uninflected quantity word (§2.4.1).
pgg-c02-e09-i2	my father's car	Genitive: a dependent noun phrase expresses possession, and written German normally prefers this construction (§2.4).
pgg-c02-e09-i5	the smell of coffee	The <i>von</i>-construction is usual when the dependent noun stands alone without a determiner (§2.4.1).
pgg-c02-e09-i6	the summit of the Matterhorn	Genitive: the dependent noun phrase identifies the summit, and the written norm prefers genitive outside listed exceptions (§2.4).
pgg-c02-e09-i8	the opinion of many Germans	Genitive: with a noun qualified by an indefinite pronoun, either genitive or a prepositional phrase is acceptable (§2.4.2).
pgg-c02-e09-i10	some of her credit balance	The <i>von</i>-construction is required in a partitive headed by an indefinite pronoun (§2.4.1).
pgg-c02-e09-i11	the end of next week	Genitive: the dependent time phrase identifies which week's end, following the written norm (§2.4).
pgg-c02-e09-i14	the improvement of my English skills	Genitive: a fully inflected dependent noun phrase normally uses the genitive in writing (§2.4).
pgg-c02-e09-i15	none of my credit balance	The <i>von</i>-construction is required in a partitive headed by an indefinite pronoun (§2.4.1).
pgg-c02-e09-i17	which of your teachers	The <i>von</i>-construction is required in a partitive headed by an indefinite pronoun (§2.4.1).
pgg-c02-e14-i1	We spoke with Maria Simon, the German film actress.	Dative: the explanatory apposition matches the case imposed on the name by the preposition (§2.6).
pgg-c02-e14-i2	My sister is a head taller than my brother.	Nominative: the comparative apposition matches the case of the subject it is compared with (§2.6).
pgg-c02-e14-i3	A statement by Özdemir, the chair of the Greens, is expected this evening.	Dative: the explanatory apposition matches the case imposed on the name by the preposition (§2.6).
pgg-c02-e14-i4	On a day like today, I would especially like to thank one man: Mr Grotewohl, the director of our company.	Dative: both the comparative and explanatory appositions match the dative phrases they describe (§2.6).
pgg-c02-e14-i5	The meeting had to be postponed until the next day, October 25.	Accusative: the date is an apposition and matches the case of the day it identifies (§2.6).
pgg-c02-e14-i6	The poet was born in 1980 in Neunkirchen an der Saar, the second-largest city in Saarland.	Dative: the explanatory apposition matches the case imposed on the place by the location preposition (§2.6).
pgg-c02-e14-i7	I would not want to be married to a man like yours.	Dative: the comparative apposition matches the case of the noun phrase it qualifies (§2.6).
pgg-c02-e14-i8	Yesterday I saw Martin Liebert, a Bavarian cabaret performer, in the restaurant.	Accusative: the explanatory apposition matches the case of the direct-object name (§2.6).
pgg-c02-e14-i10	For you as the local mayor, speaking to him as the most senior supervisory-board member must be easy. For me as an ordinary citizen, it is much harder.	The comparative appositions match their pronouns: accusative after 'for,' dative after 'with,' then accusative (§2.6).
pgg-c02-e17-i1	six bottles of German wine (the genitive form sounds old-fashioned)	Accusative: the following noun phrase is appositional and matches the measurement phrase; singular genitive is formal (§2.7.1).
pgg-c02-e17-i2	two hundred tonnes of Russian iron ore	Dative: the following singular noun phrase is appositional and matches the measurement phrase (§2.7.1).
pgg-c02-e17-i6	the price of three kilos of French apples	Dative: after the preposition, the plural noun phrase is appositional and matches the measurement phrase (§2.7.1).
pgg-c02-e17-i9	with a kind of Italian salad	Dative: the following noun phrase is appositional and matches the vague-quantity phrase (§2.7.2).
pgg-c02-e17-i10	two pounds of good coffee	Accusative: the following noun phrase is appositional and matches the direct-object measurement phrase (§2.7.1).
pgg-c02-e17-i11	I need half a liter of fresh milk.	Accusative: the following noun phrase is appositional and matches the direct-object measurement phrase (§2.7.1).

